{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["והמם — perforce, וְהָמָם is like ״וסבב בית אל״. שמם is from the same root as ״ששמם על הר ציון״. It is of the class of doubled letters, and its meaning is: 'and they raised a great noise of tumult in battle,' until destroying them. And if the meaning of the final mem were like אותם, he should have written וַהֲמָם אֹתָם. But from the derivation המה — יהמה לבי — one says וְהָמָם, and אותם [is added], as from ״עשה עצבי״ — עָשָׂם, and from ״כאשר ראה יעקב״ — ״ויאמר יעקב כאשר ראם״, i.e., 'he saw them.' וְהָמָם, from the root המה, is thus explicable as המה אותם. And if you would say to expound this one too as והמה אותם — it is impossible to say so, because the verb המיון is predicated of a person's own body: יהמה לבי — it throbs of itself. And if the verb were to produce the action upon others, it would require a dagesh: וְהִמֵּם, like ״כָּלָה שארי״ — of itself — versus ״כִלָּם״ — upon others. ״כִלָּה ה' את חמתו״ — the ketiv is defective and therefore [the form] is weak; [the subject acts] of itself. But when upon others it says וְשִׂנָּא את בגדי כלאו, וַיְשַׁנֶּהָ ואת נערותיה."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rashbam, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The text is dense and at points elliptical; Rashbam's argument is that והמם cannot mean 'and He bewildered them' (= אותם) because the stem המם/המה is intransitive (lit. 'to roar/throb'), and a transitive causative would require a dagesh in the nun-equivalent (i.e., pi'el וְהִמֵּם). The final sentence is especially compressed and may carry textual difficulties; rendered hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:38:38.387175009Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}